[{"category":"section","location":"#home-section","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Github) (Image: v0.1.0)\n\n\n\nA set of numerical methods (Gauss-Chebyshev quadrature) for coupled fracture problems\n\nDocumentation for GaussChebyshevFracture.","title":"GaussChebyshevFracture"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"tip: Useful reference\nThe notation and algorithms implemented were drawn from the following reference:Viesca, R.C. and Garagash, D. Numerical methods for coupled fracture problems. Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids. 113, 13-34, 2018. doi:10.1016/j.jmps.2018.01.008.\n\nThis package provides utilities to solve crack problems which are represented as integral equations of the (non-dimensional) form:\n\nsigmaleft(xright) = sigma_0left(xright) + frac1pi int_-1^+1 Kleft(x - sright) fracddeltads ds\n\nwhere x is the coorditate along which a straight crack lies, sigma is the stress component whose traction is oriented eigher normal or parallel to the crack face, delta is the relative displacement (difference in displacement of each side of the crack face, opening or slip) and sigma_0 is the background stress or the stress in the absence of relative displacement. Kleft(xiright) is a kernel whose most singular term will be frac1xi. \n\nThis formulation arise in problems of in-plane, anti-plane, or axisymmetric rupture (opening or sliding) of a linear elastic material. For a non-exhaustive list of problems, we refer to the tests.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"You can install GaussChebyshevFracture directly from the Pkg REPL:\n\npkg> add GaussChebyshevFracture","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Illustrative-example","page":"Home","text":"For this example, let's look at a crack subject to uniform stress drop, as described in section 3.1 of Viesca and Garagash (2018).\n\nLet's consider a traction-free crack of length 2a under a uniform far-field loading sigma_0. The dimensional form of the problem is\n\nsigmaleft(xright) = sigma_0 - fracmu^prime2 pi int_-a^+a frac1x - s fracd deltad s  ds\n\nwhere sigmaleft(xright) = 0 for leftxright  a and the modulus mu^prime = fracmu1 - nu for modes I and II (plane strain), and mu^prime = mu for mode III, mu being the shear modulus and nu the Poisson's ratio of the medium hosting the crack. delta is the displacement discontinuity on the crack surface.\n\nAfter non-dimensionalization with fracxa rightarrow x and fracdelta mu^prime2 sigma_0a rightarrow delta, the problem becomes:\n\n1 = frac1pi int_-1^+1 frac1x - sfracddeltads ds text for  leftxright  1\n\nAnticipating, due to the crack singularity, that fracddeltads sim frac1sqrt1 - leftsright as leftsright rightarrow 1, we look for a solution of the form fracddeltads = fracFleft(sright)sqrt1 - s^2, the Gaussian quadrature for the singular integral implies the discretization:\n\n1 approx frac1pi sum_j=1^n w_j fracFleft(s_jright)x_i - s_j\n\nFor this configuration, we can use \n\nusing GaussChebyshevFracture\nusing Plots\nusing LinearAlgebra\n\n# Number of quadrature points\nn = 100\n\n# Gauss-Chebyshec quadrature of 1st kind\ngc = GaussChebyshev(n, 1)\nGaussChebyshev{Float64}(GaussChebyshevFracture.var\"#40#41\"(), 100, [-0.9995065603657316, -0.9980267284282716, -0.99556196460308, -0.9921147013144778, -0.9876883405951377, -0.9822872507286887, -0.9759167619387473, -0.968583161128631, -0.960293685676943, -0.9510565162951535  …  0.9510565162951535, 0.9602936856769431, 0.9685831611286311, 0.9759167619387474, 0.9822872507286887, 0.9876883405951378, 0.9921147013144779, 0.99556196460308, 0.9980267284282716, 0.9995065603657316], [-0.9998766324816606, -0.9988898749619699, -0.996917333733128, -0.9939609554551796, -0.9900236577165575, -0.9851093261547739, -0.9792228106217659, -0.9723699203976766, -0.964557418457798, -0.9557930147983301  …  0.9557930147983301, 0.9645574184577981, 0.9723699203976766, 0.9792228106217657, 0.9851093261547739, 0.9900236577165575, 0.9939609554551797, 0.996917333733128, 0.99888987496197, 0.9998766324816606], [0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934  …  0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934, 0.031415926535897934], [0.42443936291690304 0.7638651802410275 … -0.0002357212506841905 7.85608149946993e-5; -0.08490882153074546 0.545752648832472 … 0.00023589593391182303 -7.861900439787821e-5; … ; -7.861900439639236e-5 0.00023589593390707653 … 0.5457526488323791 -0.08490882153074587; 7.85608149937167e-5 -0.00023572125068094252 … 0.7638651802411295 0.4244393629169129], [-0.0016245773065888354 -0.029565238370013524 … -0.03141592690633003 -0.03141592641250181; 0.00022611085929858372 -0.0019212125641265142 … -0.0314159257943016 -0.031415926782933944; … ; 2.470359624327789e-10 -7.41596335122191e-10 … -0.029494713971773126 -0.03164203739519658; -1.233961175427234e-10 3.7043207181688097e-10 … -0.001850688165885679 -0.029791349229308903])\n\nThe structure gc is the core of the package and provides several utilities, including the quadrature weights w, the evaluation points x and the quadrature points s.\n\nThe discretization yields n unknowns Fleft(s_jright) and only n-1 equations. This under-determinancy is resolved by an additional constraint.  Classically, such a constraint is the requirement of zero cumulative relative displacement, which can be expressed as:\n\n0 = deltaleft(1right) - deltaleft(-1right) = int_-1^+1 fracddeltads ds = int_-1^+1 fracFleft(sright)sqrt1 - s^2 ds approx sum_j=1^n w_j Fleft(s_jright)\n\nWe use this additional constraint to solve for the slip.\n\n# We solve the following system of equations:\n# A F = b\n# Right-end side b\nb = ones(n-1)\nb = vcat(b, 0.0)\n\n# Matrix A\nA = [gc.w[j] / (π * (gc.x[i] - gc.s[j])) for i in eachindex(gc.x), j in eachindex(gc.s)]\nA = vcat(A, gc.w')\n\n# Solutions for F(s)\nF = A \\ b\n100-element Vector{Float64}:\n  0.999876632481452\n  0.9988898749620341\n  0.9969173337331803\n  0.9939609554552339\n  0.9900236577165891\n  0.9851093261547734\n  0.9792228106218168\n  0.9723699203976874\n  0.9645574184577876\n  0.9557930147983145\n  ⋮\n -0.9645574184578097\n -0.9723699203977145\n -0.9792228106217836\n -0.985109326154776\n -0.9900236577165327\n -0.9939609554551222\n -0.9969173337331124\n -0.9988898749620427\n -0.9998766324815478\n\nThe known solution of this problem is given by:\n\ndeltaleft(xright) = sqrt1 - x^2\n\n# Solution slip\nδ = GaussChebyshevFracture.u(gc, F)\n# Analytical solution slip\nsol_δ = sqrt.(1 .- gc.x.^2)\n\nnorm((δ.-sol_δ)./sol_δ)\n2.765073522532446e-13\n\n# Plot solutions\nplot(gc.x, sol_δ; xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"δ\", label=\"Analytical\")\nscatter!(gc.x, δ,markersize=2; label=\"Numerical\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Illustrative example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Index","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"#GaussChebyshevFracture.GaussChebyshev","page":"Home","text":"struct GaussChebyshev\n\nA structure to store Gauss-Chebyshev quadrature points and other utilities.\n\nFields\n\nwf::Function : weight function\nn::Integer : number of quadrature points\nx::Vector : points\ns::Vector : quadrature points\nw::Vector : quadrature weigths\nL::Matrix : interpolation matrix\nS::Matrix : integration matrix \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussChebyshevFracture.GaussChebyshev"},{"category":"method","location":"#GaussChebyshevFracture.u-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{GaussChebyshev{T}, Vector{T}}} where T<:Real","page":"Home","text":"function u(gc::GaussChebyshev{T}, F::Vector{T})::Vector{T} where {T<:Real}\n\nReturns the solution uleft(x_iright) = sum_j=1^n S_ij Fleft(s_jright)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussChebyshevFracture.u"},{"category":"method","location":"#GaussChebyshevFracture.∇u-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{GaussChebyshev{T}, Vector{T}}} where T<:Real","page":"Home","text":"function ∇u(gc::GaussChebyshev{T}, F::Vector{T})::Vector{T} where {T<:Real}\n\nReturns the gradient of the solution nabla uleft(x_iright) = sqrt1 - x^2 sum_j=1^n L_ij Fleft(s_jright)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GaussChebyshevFracture.∇u"}]
